//! Byte-parity tests for the native cite writer (`epr flow cites`).
//!
//! The oracle these pin is `.claude/scripts/memory-kit/{cite-gen,cite-propagate,cites-migrate}.py`,
//! which this module replaces. Every expectation below was GENERATED by running that oracle against
//! the checked-in fixtures on 2026-09-10 and recorded here, so the pins survive the scripts'
//! deletion — a parity test whose oracle no longer exists must carry the oracle's answer, not a
//! call to it.
//!
//! ## The fixture corpus
//!
//! `tests/fixtures/.cite-writer/tree/` is a 21-file miniature repository: the ten documents whose
//! `cite-gen --seal` fixed-point digests station three recorded, at their recorded pre-seal state
//! (verbatim repository bytes — the corpus is AT its fixed point, so pre-seal and post-seal are the
//! same bytes, which the `pristine` leg asserts), plus the 11 documents their envelopes resolve to,
//! reduced to frontmatter-only stubs. The stubs are safe: `seal` recomputes a fingerprint only when
//! CONVERTING a legacy cite, and none of the ten cites a stubbed target by path, so every recorded
//! digest reproduces exactly — verified against the oracle in the same tree.
//!
//! The directory name starts with a dot ON PURPOSE. Two of the fixtures are `CLAUDE.md` files
//! carrying live `id:` slugs, and the gospel walk (in both the oracle and [`super`]) prunes
//! dot-prefixed directories — so the fixture cannot shadow the real gospel it was copied from in
//! the live slug index.

use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
use std::process::Command;

use sha2::{Digest, Sha256};

